---
import ArticleLayout from "../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro";
import QuickAnswer from "../components/QuickAnswer.astro";
import Callout from "../components/Callout.astro";

const breadcrumbs = [
  { label: "Portugal Guide", href: "/portugal-guide" },
  { label: "Practical Guides", href: "/practical-guides" },
  { label: "What to Photograph at Pickup" },
];

const toc = [
  { label: "Why this matters in Portugal", id: "why" },
  { label: "The exterior checklist", id: "exterior" },
  { label: "The interior and extras", id: "interior" },
  { label: "The paperwork", id: "paperwork" },
  { label: "At drop-off", id: "dropoff" },
];

const related = [
  { label: "Portugal Car Rental Insurance Explained", href: "/portugal-car-rental-insurance" },
  { label: "Deposits and Card Requirements", href: "/portugal-car-rental-deposits-and-card-requirements" },
  { label: "After-Hours Car Rental Returns in Portugal", href: "/after-hours-car-rental-returns-portugal" },
  { label: "Practical Guides", href: "/practical-guides" },
];
---

<ArticleLayout
  title="What to Photograph When Picking Up a Rental Car in Portugal | TripAxle"
  description="A photo checklist for rental car pickup in Portugal: which panels, wheels, glass and interior details to record, plus the paperwork shots that protect your deposit."
  eyebrow="Practical Guides"
  summary="Five minutes with your phone at pickup is the cheapest damage insurance there is. Exactly what to photograph, and the paperwork shots people forget."
  updated="2026-03-24"
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  toc={toc}
  related={related}
>
  <QuickAnswer>
    <p>
      Before you drive away, take a dated video walk-around plus close-ups of
      every existing mark, all four wheels, the windscreen, the roof and lower
      bumpers, the dashboard with the engine on, and the fuel gauge. Then
      photograph the signed condition report. This is what settles a disputed
      damage charge weeks later.
    </p>
  </QuickAnswer>

  <h2 id="why">Why this matters in Portugal</h2>
  <p>
    Portugal is not unusual for rental disputes, but two things make a good photo
    record worth the effort: deposits and excesses here are often large (see
    <a href="/portugal-car-rental-insurance">insurance explained</a>), and a fair
    number of bookings are with off-airport suppliers whose condition checks can
    be quick and whose damage claims sometimes arrive by email after you have
    flown home. Your own timestamped photos are the counter-evidence.
  </p>

  <h2 id="exterior">The exterior checklist</h2>
  <p>Do a slow video walk-around first, then stills of:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Each of the four corners at an angle, so every panel appears in at least one shot.</li>
    <li>Every scratch, scuff, dent or chip already on the car — with a second wider shot showing where on the car it is.</li>
    <li>All four alloy wheels and tyre sidewalls (kerb damage is a common charge).</li>
    <li>The windscreen and rear glass, straight on, for existing chips or cracks.</li>
    <li>The roof and the underside of the front and rear bumpers — the panels people never check and companies do.</li>
    <li>Wing mirrors and light clusters.</li>
    <li>The number plate visible in at least one exterior shot, to tie the photos to this car.</li>
  </ul>

  <Callout variant="tip">
    Make sure your phone camera has date stamps or location on, or take one photo
    of the day's newspaper / your phone's lock screen clock next to the car.
    Metadata is good; a visible timestamp is harder to argue with.
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="interior">The interior and extras</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Dashboard with the engine running — odometer reading, fuel level, and any warning lights.</li>
    <li>Seats, trim and boot for existing tears, stains or burns.</li>
    <li>Any child seat you were given, including its condition and that it was actually fitted.</li>
    <li>The spare wheel or tyre-repair kit and the warning triangle and hi-vis vest (legally required in Portugal) — so you can prove they were or were not present.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="paperwork">The paperwork</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The signed vehicle condition / check-out report, both sides.</li>
    <li>The rental agreement page showing the fuel policy, the excess amount, and the deposit taken.</li>
    <li>The assistance / breakdown phone number.</li>
    <li>If the check-out sheet marks existing damage with a diagram, photograph it and check it matches what you actually see.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If you spot damage that is not on the sheet, get it added and initialled
    before you leave. A photo of an un-amended sheet does not help you.
  </p>

  <h2 id="dropoff">At drop-off</h2>
  <p>
    Repeat a shorter version at the end: full walk-around video, wheels, glass,
    the odometer and fuel gauge, and — if someone inspects the car — a photo of
    them doing it and of any “no damage” sign-off. For an
    <a href="/after-hours-car-rental-returns-portugal">after-hours return</a>
    where no one checks the car, the drop-off photos are the only record that the
    car came back undamaged, so take them carefully and note the time.
  </p>
</ArticleLayout>
